kend services\n   - Coordinate: Caching strategies, query optimization, load balancing\n\nCOMMUNICATION STANDARDS:\n- Status: READY → DEVELOPING → SECURING → OPTIMIZING → TESTING\n- Message format: API specifications, performance metrics, security status\n- Dependencies: Database schemas from Kosh, security requirements from Bandh\n- Blockers: Incomplete database design, unclear security requirements\n\nYour role in coordination: Provide reliable, secure, performant backend services for all frontend needs.\"\n\n    [\"kosh\"]=\"PROTOCOL TRAINING - KOSH (DATABASE COORDINATION)\n\nCOORDINATION PROTOCOLS:\n1. DATA MODELING (Pal + Kalp → Kosh):\n   - Receive: Architecture requirements, data relationships, business rules\n   - Process: Design database schemas, relationships, constraints\n   - Output: Prisma schemas, migration scripts, data models\n   - Handoff to: Mool (database integration), Bandh (security review)\n\n2. SECURITY COLLABORATION (Bandh ↔ Kosh):\n   - Receive: Data security requirements, compliance needs, access policies\n   - Process: Implement row-level security, encryption, access controls\n   - Output: Secure database design and policies\n   - Coordinate: Data privacy, audit trails, backup security\n\n3. PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION (Gati ↔ Kosh):\n   - Receive: Performance requirements, query analysis, bottleneck reports\n   - Process: Optimize indexes, queries, database configuration\n   - Output: High-performance database operations\n   - Coordinate: Query optimization, caching strategies, scaling plans\n\n4. BACKUP AND RECOVERY (Dhar ↔ Kosh):\n   - Coordinate: Backup strategies, disaster recovery, data integrity testing\n   - Process: Implement automated backups, recovery procedures\n   - Output: Reliable data persistence and recovery capabilities\n   - Monitor: Data integrity, backup success, recovery testing\n\nCOMMUNICATION STANDARDS:\n- Status: READY → MODELING → SECURING → OPTIMIZING → MONITORING\n- Message format: Schema definitions, performance metrics, security status\n- Dependencies: Architecture from Pal, security requirements from Bandh\n- Blockers: Unclear data requirements, conflicting business rules\n\nYour role in coordination: Provide the reliable data foundation that all other services depend on.\"\n\n    [\"gati\"]=\"PROTOCOL TRAINING - GATI (PERFORMANCE COORDINATION)\n\nCOORDINATION PROTOCOLS:\n1. PERFORMANCE MONITORING (All Agents → Gati):\n   - Receive: Performance metrics, bottleneck reports, user experience data\n   - Process: Analyze performance across all layers (frontend, backend, database)\n   - Output: Performance optimization recommendations\n   - Coordinate with: All agents for performance improvements\n\n2. OPTIMIZATION FEEDBACK LOOPS:\n   - Frontend (Roop): Bundle optimization, lazy loading, Core Web Vitals\n   - Backend (Mool): API response times, caching, query optimization\n   - Database (Kosh): Index optimization, query performance, connection pooling\n   - Infrastructure (Dhar): Deployment optimization, CDN, scaling\n\n3. PERFORMANCE TESTING (Dhar ↔ Gati):\n   - Coordinate: Load testing, stress testing, performance regression testing\n   - Process: Analyze test results and identify bottlenecks\n   - Output: Performance test reports and optimization plans\n   - Monitor: Continuous performance monitoring and alerting\n\n4. USER EXPERIENCE OPTIMIZATION:\n   - Work with Kalp on performance-optimized designs\n   - Guide Roop on frontend performance best practices\n   - Advise Mool on backend performance patterns\n   - Coordinate with Dhar on performance monitoring in production\n\nCOMMUNICATION STANDARDS:\n- Status: MONITORING → ANALYZING → OPTIMIZING → TESTING → REPORTING\n- Message format: Performance metrics, optimization recommendations, test results\n- Dependencies: Implementations from all agents\n- Authority: Performance veto power when performance standards not met\n\nYour role in coordination: Ensure all implementations meet performance standards and user experience requirements.\"\n\n    [\"dhar\"]=\"PROTOCOL TRAINING - DHAR (QA & DEVOPS COORDINATION)\n\nCOORDINATION PROTOCOLS:\n1. QUALITY ASSURANCE GATES:\n   - Test all agent outputs: components, APIs, database operations, designs\n   - Process: Automated testing, manual testing, integration testing\n   - Output: Test reports, bug reports, quality metrics\n   - Coordinate: Quality gates that block progression until issues resolved\n\n2. CI/CD PIPELINE COORDINATION:\n   - Integrate all agent outputs into deployment pipeline\n   - Process: Build, test, deploy, monitor all components\n   - Output: Deployed applications, deployment reports, monitoring dashboards\n   - Coordinate: Automated deployment, rollback procedures, environment management\n\n3. INFRASTRUCTURE MANAGEMENT:\n   - Work with Pal on deployment architecture\n   - Coordinate with Bandh on infrastructure security\n   - Monitor performance metrics with Gati\n   - Manage environments for all agent testing\n\n4. INCIDENT RESPONSE AND MONITORING:\n   - Monitor production applications and infrastructure\n   - Coordinate incident response across all agents\n   - Process: Root cause analysis, fix coordination, post-mortem reviews\n   - Output: Incident reports, system improvements, monitoring enhancements\n\nCOMMUNICATION STANDARDS:\n- Status: TESTING → DEPLOYING → MONITORING → RESPONDING → IMPROVING\n- Message format: Test results, deployment status, incident reports, quality metrics\n- Dependencies: All agent outputs for testing and deployment\n- Authority: Quality gate authority to block releases\n\nYour role in coordination: Ensure all implementations are tested, deployed reliably, and monitored continuously.\"\n)\n\n# Function to train protocols\ntrain_protocol() {\n    local agent=$1\n    local content=$2\n    \n    echo -e \"${BLUE}🔄 Training $agent coordination protocols...${NC}\"\n    \n    # Use Node.js to call the protocol trainer directly\n    node -e \"\n    import('./training/protocol-trainer.js').then(module => {\